{"name":"Lantern Light","hex":"#f6ebb9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lantern","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A case of translucent or transparent material made to protect a flame, or light, used to illuminate its surroundings.","Especially, a metal casing with lens used to illuminate a stage (e.g. spotlight, floodlight)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish with a lantern."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lantern"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lantern","blocks":[{"text":"A lantern is a source of lighting, often portable. It typically features a protective enclosure for the light source – historically usually a candle, a wick in oil, or a heated mesh, and often a battery-powered light in modern times – to make it easier to carry and hang up, and make it more reliable outdoors or in drafty interiors. Lanterns may also be used for signaling, as torches, or as general light-sources outdoors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/CNW_brakeman%27s_kerosene_lantern.JPG/330px-CNW_brakeman%27s_kerosene_lantern.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lantern","wikidata":"Q862454"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Arabic_lanterns.jpg/500px-Arabic_lanterns.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Arabic_lanterns.jpg/1280px-Arabic_lanterns.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Arabic_lanterns.jpg/500px-Arabic_lanterns.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Arabic style lanterns (fanous), symbolic in the Islamic month of Ramadan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArabic_lanterns.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/OHM_-_Geburt_Christi_1e.jpg/500px-OHM_-_Geburt_Christi_1e.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/OHM_-_Geburt_Christi_1e.jpg/1280px-OHM_-_Geburt_Christi_1e.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/OHM_-_Geburt_Christi_1e.jpg/500px-OHM_-_Geburt_Christi_1e.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"15th-century candle lantern from Germany, perforated metal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOHM_-_Geburt_Christi_1e.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Fuenteplazaalta.JPG/500px-Fuenteplazaalta.JPG?utm_source=en.wik